* {{hyphenation|en|pot|latch}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|es}}

# {{lb|en|Canada|US|also|figuratively}} A [[ceremony]] amongst certain [[indigenous]] [[person|peoples]] of the [[Pacific Northwest]] in which [[gift#Noun|gifts]] are [[bestow]]ed upon [[guest]]s and [[personal property]] is [[destroy]]ed in a [[show#Noun|show]] of [[generosity]] and [[wealth]].
#* '''1900''', {{w|Jack London}}, ''[[s:The Son of the Wolf|The Son of the Wolf]]''
#*:That night there was a grand wedding and a '''potlach'''; so that for two days to follow there was no fishing done by the village. 
# {{lb|en|US|chiefly|Alaska}} A [[communal]] [[meal]] to which guests bring [[dish#Noun|dishes]] to [[share#Verb|share]]; a [[potluck]].
#: {{synonyms|en|fuddle|q1=Britain, dialectal}}
#* '''2015''', Yuval Noah Harari, ''Sapiens: A Brief History of Humankind'':
#*: If all went according to plan, the bands could harvest tons of meat, fat and animal skins in a single afternoon of collective effort, and either consume these riches in a giant '''potlatch''', or dry, smoke or (in Arctic areas) freeze them for later usage.

